<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1671">1671</a>–<a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as John Law (economist)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Law was a Scottish economist and financier who became controller-general of finances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1716 he founded the Banque Générale, later the Banque Royale, which issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, and he headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-company">Mississippi Company</a>, which controlled trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. Speculation in its shares produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> Bubble, whose collapse in 1720 ruined many investors and forced Law to flee France; he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p></div>
